@flexiarg or3/ask-first-then-bring-the-expert
@title Ask First, Then Bring the Expert
@audience OR coordinators, seminar convenors, researcher developers
@tone formal-analytic
@style design-pattern
@sigils [👂/问 🎤/专]
@see or3/coordinator-in-your-school, or3/openness-without-exposure

! conclusion: Ask First, Then Bring the Expert

  + context: You are responsible for building open research capability in a department, and you can invite speakers.

  + IF:
    You have a seminar slot, a small budget for visitors, and colleagues at very different starting points.

  + HOWEVER:
    The default programme is a general introduction to open science, and the people who attend it are the people already convinced. Everyone else has a specific, unglamorous blocker — a method that seems incompatible, a data type that cannot be shared — and hears the general case as confirmation that this is not for them.

  + THEN:
    Survey the department for expressed needs first, then commission speakers against those needs specifically, addressing the details of real projects rather than the principles. Where a subgroup believes openness cannot apply to their work, make that the topic and bring in someone who does it in that tradition. Pitch the sessions as "here's why I did this and why it might be useful to you" rather than as training — and add practical, applied sessions afterwards, once awareness has turned into willingness.

  + BECAUSE:
    People know what they do not know, and a session aimed at a question someone actually asked converts scepticism into a next action. The general introduction confirms priors in both directions; the specific answer removes an actual obstacle.

  + NEXT-STEPS:
    next[Run a short needs assessment before booking any speaker; let the answers set the programme.]
    next[Track whether attendance changes practice — the honest gap in most such programmes — with light-touch follow-up rather than attendance counts.]
    next[Co-host at least one session with another unit; the model travels and the audience widens.]
